# Copy to `.env` on the deployment host, then fill in the token below.
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env
#
# This is the *deployment* env file, read by `docker compose`. It is not the
# same thing as `.env.example`, which documents the variables the API takes when
# you run it directly from a checkout. Everything the container itself needs is
# already set in its `Dockerfile` and in its compose fragment; what is here is
# the tunnel credential, which cannot be committed, the choice of which released
# version this host runs, and the list of fragments it merges.
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Keep it that way.

# --- Which tools this host runs ----------------------------------------------
# **The list of fragments is the only place a host says which tools it runs**,
# and `COMPOSE_FILE` is where that list lives so it is written once rather than
# retyped as `-f` flags on every command — including the ones typed during a
# rollback, by someone who is not reading this file. With it set, this host's
# commands are a bare `docker compose pull` and `docker compose up -d`.
#
# `docs/adr/004-one-compose-fragment-per-tool.md` is the decision; the fragments
# are:
#
#   compose.downloader.yaml        the downloader, built from source
#   compose.downloader.prod.yaml   its released image, its TRUST_PROXY
#   compose.planner.yaml           the planner's routing engine and geocoder
#   compose.planner.prod.yaml      the planner's released image
#   compose.prod.yaml              cloudflared and the edge network — no tool
#
# The line below is a downloader-only host: TUNNEL_TOKEN, GHCR_OWNER and
# DOWNLOADER_TAG below are the three it needs. Then, in order:
#
#   ...:compose.planner.prod.yaml
#       the planner too, on the same tunnel. Adds PLANNER_TAG.
#   ...:compose.planner.yaml
#       and the planner's routing engine, geocoder and discovery, so its
#       distances are real rather than fixtures. Adds OSM_EXTRACT_URL and
#       NOMINATIM_PASSWORD. This one is hours of CPU — read the section below,
#       and docs/02-DEPLOYMENT.md, before merging it.
#
# A planner-only host drops the two `downloader` entries and keeps
# `compose.prod.yaml`, which names no tool. The separator is `:` on Linux and
# `;` on Windows; `COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR` overrides it. An explicit `-f` on the
# command line replaces this list rather than adding to it, which is what makes
# `docker compose -f compose.planner.yaml --profile tiles run ...` still mean
# only that file.
#
# **Every fragment sets `name: webtools`**, so any merge of them is one compose
# project. If this host has been running from a checkout whose directory was not
# called `webtools`, its volumes are filed under that basename and it needs the
# one-time migration in docs/02-DEPLOYMENT.md **before** its next `up -d` — a
# renamed project comes up looking like a fresh install with the real data still
# on disk under a name nothing references.
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.downloader.yaml:compose.prod.yaml:compose.downloader.prod.yaml

# --- Cloudflare Tunnel -------------------------------------------------------
# Issued by the Zero Trust dashboard when you create the tunnel, and shown once:
#   Networks → Tunnels → Create a tunnel → Cloudflared → copy the token out of
#   the install command it offers you (the long string after `--token`).
#
# It is a bearer credential for the tunnel, not an identifier. Anyone holding it
# can serve traffic on your hostnames. If it leaks, delete the tunnel in the
# dashboard and make a new one — rotating it is not a thing.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# --- Which version this host runs --------------------------------------------
# The images are published to GHCR by `.github/workflows/release.yml` when a
# release pull request is merged. Deploying is choosing a version here and
# running `docker compose pull && up -d`; rolling back is putting the previous
# one back and doing the same. Both are covered in docs/03-RELEASING.md.
#
# The GitHub account or organisation that owns the repository, lowercased —
# GHCR paths are case sensitive and GitHub logins are not.
GHCR_OWNER=

# An exact version, e.g. 0.2.0. Deliberately not defaulted to `latest`: a host
# that follows a moving tag cannot tell you what it is running, and the answer
# changes underneath you on the next pull. The released versions are the tags on
# the repository (`downloader-v0.2.0`) and the entries in the tool's CHANGELOG.
DOWNLOADER_TAG=

# The same, for the planner, and only read by `compose.planner.prod.yaml`. A
# downloader-only host never merges that file and can leave this empty; a host
# that does merge it and leaves this empty gets a refusal naming the variable,
# not a service that starts on a tag nobody chose.
#
# The two tools release independently — `separate-pull-requests` in
# `release-please-config.json` — so these versions are unrelated numbers and
# one of them moving is not a reason to move the other.
PLANNER_TAG=

# --- Grounding the planner ---------------------------------------------------
# Only read by `compose.planner.yaml`, and only needed on a host that merges it.
# A downloader-only host can ignore this whole section.
#
# The planner ships with GROUNDING_PROVIDER=fixtures, which reaches nothing and
# answers from a checked-in table. Making its distances real means standing up a
# routing engine and a geocoder — Valhalla routes and does not geocode, so it is
# two services — and building a graph from one OSM extract. The whole procedure,
# including what a rebuild costs and why not to automate it, is in
# docs/02-DEPLOYMENT.md.

# One .osm.pbf from Geofabrik, and the only line the two services agree about.
# Take the smallest region that contains the trips this instance will plan:
# everything downstream — download, build time, scratch space, memory — scales
# with it. A province is a comfortable afternoon; a continent is not.
OSM_EXTRACT_URL=

# Internal to the geocoder's own container. Nothing outside it ever sees this,
# and no service is published to a host port, but it has no default because a
# shipped default password is a habit worth not having.
NOMINATIM_PASSWORD=

# Set to True for the one run that rebuilds an existing graph against a fresher
# extract. Left False so a routine `up` can never start hours of CPU by
# accident.
# VALHALLA_FORCE_REBUILD=False

# Neither image was pulled when compose.planner.yaml was written — pl-28 was
# built with no Docker and no registry — so verify these against the projects'
# own release pages and pin them. Same warning `cloudflared:latest` carries: a
# `pull` can otherwise change a routing engine under a working deployment.
# VALHALLA_IMAGE_TAG=3.7.0
# NOMINATIM_IMAGE_TAG=4.4

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything else you would otherwise pass to a service can go here and be
# referenced from whichever fragment defines it.
